  free here: it requires picking a module story — [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m with ES imports, a bundler, or a plain global
  attached to [38;5;153mwindow[39m. That decision affects every form that later consumes the tracker, so it belongs in the spec. I'll
  bring you a recommendation on it after the next answer.

  [1mThe fork now:[0m what the tracking data is [3mfor[0m. This is upstream of where it gets stored, because the purpose sets the
  durability bar — and storage is the expensive thing to change after records exist.

  - [1mDebugging / development visibility[0m — you want to see what happened during a session. [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is enough:
    survives reload, zero infrastructure, inspectable in devtools. Costs nothing, rules out nothing, trivially
    replaceable.
  - [1mProduct analytics[0m — you want aggregate behavior across users. Needs records leaving the browser eventually, so it
    implies a backend or a third-party endpoint. [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m can buffer until then, so you can start client-side
    without redoing the record shape.
  - [1mAudit trail / security[0m — you want a trustworthy record of who logged in. This one can't be client-side at all:
    [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is user-editable and user-clearable, so a login audit log kept there is worth nothing against the
    threat it exists for. Requires a server, which this repo doesn't have.

  [1mMy recommendation: tell me it's the first or second, and I'll design a record shape plus a [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m-backed store [0m
  [1mwith a flush seam[0m — that serves debugging today and upgrades to analytics without changing callers. If it's the third,
  the real work is standing up a backend and the client piece is the small half; I'd want to design that as its own
  spec.
